# CACHE POLICY - added 2026-08-02 after proving a deployed JS fix was not reaching browsers.
#
# Measured on the live site: the cached copy of services.js was 42,801 bytes and did NOT contain a
# change that had already deployed; a cache-busted fetch of the SAME URL returned 45,707 bytes and
# DID. One URL, two different files, and every returning visitor got the stale one.
#
# The CSS had been given a manual ?v=<number> buster. The JS never was, and nothing stamps one at
# build time - so every client-side fix shipped this year could be sitting behind a stale cache,
# including the cart repair and the dashboard ticket list. The code was correct, deployed, and
# simply not running. "Pushed" was never the same thing as "live".
#
# max-age=0, must-revalidate keeps the file cached but forces revalidation against the origin: an
# unchanged asset costs a 304, a changed one is picked up on the very next load. That is the right
# trade for a site that ships fixes far more often than it serves traffic - and unlike a version
# query string it cannot be forgotten on the one deploy that mattered.
/assets/css/*
  Content-Type: text/css; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/assets/js/*
  Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.html
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
